# quickshell.service — this repo's FIRST custom systemd --user unit (18-07,
# QBAR-10, D-18-40).
#
# Every autostart entry in this repo — the wallpaper daemon, the (now
# retired) bar, its fullscreen watcher, quickshell (until this plan), the
# notification daemon retired in Phase 19, the (now retired) OSD daemon,
# the (now retired, RETIRE-06) standalone media applet, elephant, walker,
# hypridle, the polkit agent and both clipboard watchers — launches
# through a transient `uwsm app` scope in
# `app-graphical.slice`, with no `Restart=` directive anywhere. There is
# zero precedent in this repo for a custom systemd `--user` unit before
# this file; the only stowed systemd artifact that existed until now was a
# drop-in overriding the PACKAGED unit of the notification daemon Phase
# 19 retired, not an authored one. This
# unit is a deliberate, scoped exception: quickshell alone gets a
# supervisor, because 18-01 made it the first surface in this repo that is
# permanently mounted — its process death is now a visible desktop outage,
# not the invisible loss of a headless probe root, and from Phase 19 the
# same process also carries the notification server. Widening this
# deviation to any other autostart entry is explicitly out of scope for
# this plan.
#
# Shape copied from the retired bar's own packaged unit (inspected via
# `systemctl --user cat`, packaged under /usr/lib/systemd/user/,
# `disabled`; exact unit name recorded in 18-20-SUMMARY.md's
# scrubbed-history section): PartOf=/After= graphical-session.target,
# Restart=on-failure. That file carried a restart directive all along
# while the bar it supervised had actually been running from a uwsm scope
# the whole time — this unit therefore outlives it; RETIRE-02 (18-20)
# deleted that bar's package (and its packaged unit with it).
#
# Why ExecStart= is the existing launcher script and not the quickshell
# binary directly: quickshell-launch.sh (unchanged by this plan's Task 2
# below the header) owns the binary/config existence guards, ~1MiB log
# rotation, the measured QSG_RENDER_LOOP=threaded export (6ms vs 16ms
# frame time on this host), and the startup marker quickshell-doctor
# greps out of ~/.cache/quickshell.log. Bypassing the script to exec
# quickshell directly would silently break that shipped checker and lose
# a measured performance setting — the script stays the exec target so
# the cutover is invisible to everything already checking it.
#
# The restart contract, in plain words (systemd.service(5), read directly
# on this host): only a non-zero exit or a signal NOT in the clean-exit
# exemption list restarts the unit. SIGHUP, SIGINT, SIGTERM, SIGPIPE and a
# clean `exit 0` do NOT restart it. Two consequences that matter here:
# (1) quickshell-launch.sh's two guard paths (missing binary, missing
# shell.qml) both `exit 0` on purpose, so a missing binary or config
# degrades to a logged skip and can never enter a restart cycle; (2) a
# plain `pkill quickshell` sends SIGTERM by default, which will NOT bring
# the bar back — the single most likely misdiagnosis this unit can cause.
# The correct restart command is `systemctl --user restart
# quickshell.service`.
#
# The chosen numbers, and why neither is the systemd default (measured
# live on this host: RestartSec default is 100ms;
# DefaultStartLimitIntervalUSec=10s, DefaultStartLimitBurst=5):
#
#   RestartSec=2 (not 100ms) — a genuine one-off crash is back before the
#   user reaches for anything at two seconds, while a crash-looping build
#   burns roughly half a start per second instead of ten, giving each
#   attempt's PipeWire and D-Bus connections time to actually tear down
#   before the next one opens more (RESEARCH.md Pitfall 9's secondary
#   effect).
#
#   StartLimitIntervalSec=60 / StartLimitBurst=5 (not the default 10s
#   window) — at the DEFAULT window, five cycles of a 2-second backoff
#   plus even half a second of process life already exceed 10 seconds, so
#   a build that survives half a second per attempt would NEVER trip the
#   limit at all — the exact unbounded loop this decision exists to
#   foreclose. At 60 seconds, any crash cycle shorter than roughly twelve
#   seconds trips the limit within about ten seconds of session start and
#   the unit stops — stopped and inspectable via `systemctl --user
#   status` — rather than respawning forever; a rarer crash still
#   recovers indefinitely.
#
# Three deliberate divergences from the retired bar's packaged unit
# shape, each a considered omission rather than an oversight:
#
#   1. No [Install] section, and therefore no enable step. Enabling would
#      write a wants-symlink into the user's own systemd tree that never
#      lands in the repo, contradicting this project's core value that
#      the whole setup reproduces from a clone plus one script. Omitting
#      the stanza makes that failure mode structurally impossible — the
#      unit cannot be enabled even by accident (`systemctl --user
#      is-enabled` reports `static`). autostart.lua starts it explicitly
#      instead, keeping that file the single readable inventory of what a
#      session launches.
#
#   2. No hard `Requisite` directive on the session target. The retired
#      bar's packaged unit carried one. Measured on this host: graphical-session.target and the
#      compositor's own autostart handler both timestamp inside the same
#      one-second tick, so a hard requirement is a coin-flip between a
#      running bar and a start refused for a session a fraction of a
#      second from ready. The ordering directive (After=) is kept — it
#      costs nothing and cannot fail. PartOf= is what actually matters
#      here and IS kept: it is what stops this unit at session end, which
#      is in turn what stops the restart policy from retrying forever
#      against a compositor that no longer exists.
#
#   3. No ExecReload=. The retired bar's packaged unit mapped reload onto
#      a signal because that was its whole style-reload mechanism. Quickshell
#      hot-reloads its QML on file change natively, and the visibility
#      actuation seam is an IPC call arriving in 18-15 — inventing a
#      reload verb here would create a second, untested control path for
#      something no caller asks for.
#
# Slice=app-graphical.slice is not decoration: it puts the supervised
# process in the same cgroup parent the uwsm scope used before this
# cutover, so nothing that targets that slice — resource control, session
# teardown — changes behaviour across the switch.

[Unit]
Description=Quickshell shell root (bar, panels, overview)
PartOf=graphical-session.target
After=graphical-session.target
StartLimitIntervalSec=60
StartLimitBurst=5

[Service]
Type=simple
Slice=app-graphical.slice
ExecStart=%h/.config/hypr/scripts/quickshell-launch.sh
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=2
